{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact abedf96148155eaa6a78bd8ed0fa03a28d298354, all 24 changed paths versus main 1f7a7a1e, S2 results-note artifact first. Reviewed in a separate detached checkout: npm ci, npm ci --prefix experiments/pds, npm run check (0), npm test (179/179), npm run test:pds (22/22, 0 skipped) against the official PDS 0.5.31 on loopback. Independent adversarial static pass plus my own reading of the sequencer, PDS client, lease, service and source store.\n\nRequired before approval:\n1. Conditional persistence can silently become unconditional. PdsClient.latestCommit never checks that the returned cid is a string, so a 200 body without cid makes the before-and-after bracket in readSnapshot compare undefined with undefined and pass, snapshot.commit becomes undefined, and PdsClient.apply omits swapCommit when it is falsy. The entry-plus-head batch is then written unconditionally; provisionLog has the same shape. An honest PDS never does this, so it needs a faulty intermediary or a bypassed lease to matter, but the S2 gate's core property is that every append is conditional on the exact observed commit, and the code must not be able to drop that condition on a malformed reply. Validate cid and rev as non-empty strings in latestCommit and make swapCommit mandatory on the append and provision paths, with a fixture that feeds a cid-less getLatestCommit reply through the fault proxy and expects a refusal, never a write.\n2. docs/pds-host.md describes recovery after the PDS drops a dereferenced blob as \"recovery requires retained bytes\", and the note presents it as a procedure, but SourceStore.put rethrows every get failure except RecordNotFound, so ContentUnavailable and a tampered reference propagate and the host has no code path that uploads retained bytes before restoring the record; the test performs that recovery by hand with raw upload and applyWrites. Either implement the repair in put or state plainly in the doc and note that recovery is manual in S2.\n\nNon-blocking, for the same recut or S3: the lost-response test asserts position and count but not the proxy's observations, so it does not itself prove one upstream write and no duplicate attempt; no test runs two Sequencer instances on one log with the lease bypassed, so PDS-level exclusion rests on swapCommit alone; every run leaves a sequencer private key and access token in .atseq-local/pds-*/writer-secrets.json (0600, ignored, never printed or archived) with no cleanup; the lease catches every SQLite error as \"another process holds the lease\"; RecordAlreadyExists is reconciled but PDS 0.5.31 never emits it; npm test already includes tests/pds.test.ts so the documented reproduction runs the PDS gate twice; a killed test runner can orphan the forked PDS on an ephemeral loopback port, which resetDisposable then refuses (fails safe); and the npm audit count in docs/pds-host.md will drift.\n\nVerified: one applyWrites per attempt carries the entry create and the head update with swapCommit equal to the bracketed observed commit, and the PDS checks the swap before any operation inside one actor-store transaction; position comes from the verified head, never a counter; InvalidSwap, 5xx and transport errors all re-read and retry the same signed intent, the retry index is rebuilt from retained entries on every pass, and a receipt is returned only after the entry is found in verified persistent history, so a lost reply resolves to the original receipt across process death; rollback and fork are detected against the retained head; the SQLite exclusive lease is kernel-released on SIGKILL and honestly documented as local only; source blobs are retained through record references with CID, shape, hash and size checks on read and a 512 KiB bound both ways; every listed record's CID and position key are recomputed, verifyHistory orders by signed position, and PDS cid, rev and cursor are used only as equality checks, the swap token and bounded pagination; the service binds loopback with an ephemeral port, refuses any Origin, caps bodies at 128 KiB, validates input, params and output against the Lexicons, and submit performs only intent verification; isolation uses a marked disposable directory under the checkout, ephemeral ports, a refusal to reset a running PDS, and no silent skip; all 17 source hashes in experiments/pds.json match HEAD, 21 scenarios plus the parent is 22, PDS 0.5.31 matches the isolated lockfile, and no secrets appear in the evidence. The gate-to-test mapping is complete for every S2 bullet.\n\nArchitecture: host layer added (architecture sections 5 and 6: canonical writes and delivery); protocol, runtime, definition and interaction layers unchanged; interpretation stays S3 and the note says so. Security: finding 1 is a response-validation gap on the PDS trust boundary that can remove the write condition; finding 2 is a documented recovery the host does not perform. Everything else on the boundary is verified above. Simplification: nothing to cut beyond the dead RecordAlreadyExists branch and the duplicate gate invocation in the reproduction steps.\n\nOn the recut: republish all path artifacts at the new head with the results note first and request fresh review. Nothing here adopts the plan-review recommendations.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:f5b900eb95adbaa6da81c8957f64dad5fe8114bf","head":"abedf96148155eaa6a78bd8ed0fa03a28d298354","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:c3537c3d09a8e621baf6dbaabdb7ba546734f986\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:c3537c3d09a8e621baf6dbaabdb7ba546734f986","review_path":"reviewguard@1","stale":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:01480ae861886a9aeeec8c78ffe7f1ae000790dd, git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:1e068c987b1f373c421817cf67033f59e449e3ae, git:sha